<Layout title="GDev Academy — Nikita Ostroukhov" description="Nikita Ostroukhov’s GDev Academy game development channel.">
  <header><h1>GDev Academy</h1><p class="subtitle">A YouTube channel about game development. 12,000+ subscribers.</p></header>
  <section>
    <p>
      I started <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@GDevAcademy">GDev Academy</a> as a distribution channel for my indie game
      development projects. The idea was that educational content had a much
      longer discovery lifetime than direct product promotion, while a
      game-development audience provided a natural place to introduce the games
      we were building.
    </p>
    <p>
      The channel grew organically to more than 12,000 subscribers. I was
      responsible for the overall concept and direction of the project, while a content producer 
      handled most of the day-to-day video production.
    </p>
    <p>
      I made only one <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h85HMg3V_Rk">video</a> for the channel entirely by myself, from concept and script to recording 
      and editing. It reached more than 200,000 views and was the clearest expression of the original idea 
      behind the channel: educational content built around a product I wanted to promote. The video coincided 
      with the release of <a href="/projects/lock-the-fox">Lock the Fox</a> and featured the game throughout, which is why I chose to make this particular 
      video myself rather than delegate it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Despite the audience growth, the channel generated less commercial value
      for the games than I had expected. I eventually stopped developing it as a
      business project and handed the channel over to the content producer, who
      continues to use it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p><a href="/">← Back to home</a></p>
</Layout>
